<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 825 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">Obelisk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> is a black limestone monument from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, erected around 825 BCE and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Its relief panels show tribute brought to the Assyrian king from five lands; one panel shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a> of Israel, or his envoy, prostrate before Shalmaneser, the only known contemporary image of a king of Israel or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-obelisk-of-shalmaneser-iii.jpg" alt="Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III,_9th_century_BC,_from_Nimrud,_Iraq._The_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
